<article class="container pt-10 pb-5">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <h1 class="pb-3">Network and edge</h1>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">From fibre to the office and the house</h2>
            <p>
                The HomeLab connects a 2 Gbit/s fibre service to a NanoPi R6S running FriendlyWrt. The WAN link negotiates at 2.5 GbE, and the two LAN ports feed the office and the house.
                To mirror the setup, connect the office’s three 2.5 GbE switches in sequence for the Proxmox cluster and development workstations.
                Connect the house’s four switches in the order shown below and run the three Tenda Nova Wi-Fi mesh nodes in bridge mode.
            </p>
            <pre class="bg-light rounded-3 p-3"><code>2 Gbit/s fibre
└── NanoPi R6S (2.5 GbE WAN) / FriendlyWrt
    ├── Docker
    │   ├── Primary PI-Hole/Unbound
    │   └── NGinx Proxy Manager
    ├── Office (2.5 GbE LAN)
    │   └── Entry 5 port 2.5 GbE switch
    │       └── Desk 5 port 2.5 GbE switch
    │           └── Back 5 port 2.5 GbE switch
    └── House (1 GbE LAN)
        ├── Router 5 port 1 GbE switch
        │   └── TV 5 port 2.5 GbE switch
        │       ├── Main/Living Room Wi-Fi mesh node (in bridge mode)
        │       └── Kitchen 8 port 2.5 GbE switch
        │           ├── Second/Kitchen Wi-Fi mesh node
        │           └── Upstairs 5 port 2.5 GbE switch
        │               └── Third/Upsatirs Wi-Fi mesh node
        └── Secondary PI-Hole/Unbound</code></pre>
            <p>
                Put both wired branches on the same LAN bridge and use FriendlyWrt as the default gateway, firewall and DHCP server. Configure DHCP to advertise both Pi-hole addresses.
                The house shares a 1 GbE uplink for traffic to the internet or office, even though some links farther down the chain support 2.5 GbE.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Maintaining FriendlyWrt on the NanoPi R6S</h2>
            <p>
                Choose FriendlyElec’s Docker-capable FriendlyWrt build to run Pi-hole and the reverse proxy on the R6S.
                Before upgrading, record the running firmware, driver and installed packages. The commands below assume <code>eth2</code> is the WAN interface. Confirm the interface mapping on the router first.
                The <a href="https://wiki.friendlyelec.com/wiki/index.php/NanoPi_R6S">NanoPi R6S wiki</a> covers port mapping, eMMC Tools and recovery.
                Firmware and checksums are available from <a href="https://github.com/friendlyarm/Actions-FriendlyWrt/releases">FriendlyWrt releases</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">ubus call system board
uname -a
ip -br link
ethtool eth2
ethtool -i eth2
apk info &gt; /tmp/friendlywrt-packages.txt
sysupgrade -b /tmp/friendlywrt-config.tar.gz
tar -tzf /tmp/friendlywrt-config.tar.gz</code></pre>
            <p>
                Copy the configuration archive from <code>sysupgrade -b</code> and the package list off the router. Back up the Docker stacks and their persistent data separately,
                and have a bootable recovery microSD card ready. Before uploading the image, compare its SHA-256 with the value published for that exact release.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">sha256sum ./friendlywrt-r6s-image.tgz</code></pre>
            <p>
                To upgrade the eMMC installation, open <strong>System → eMMC Tools</strong> in LuCI and select the FriendlyElec archive for the R6S and its partition layout.
                The vendor tool offers a data-preserving option for compatible minor upgrades. The lab’s last upgrade booted with factory settings, which required selectively
                restoring network and service settings and remounting the Docker data. After rebooting, check the kernel and Ethernet driver again, then test WAN routing, both DNS servers and HTTPS.
            </p>
            <p>
                FriendlyWrt 25.12 uses <code>apk</code>. Run <code>apk update</code> to refresh the package index and
                <code>apk list --upgradable</code> to see which packages have updates. For a system-wide update, use a compatible firmware image so the kernel, Realtek 2.5 GbE driver and packages come from the same build. See the
                <a href="https://openwrt.org/docs/guide-user/additional-software/apk">OpenWrt APK guide</a>.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Two Pi-hole DNS servers</h2>
            <p>
                Run the primary Pi-hole in Docker on the NanoPi and place its Unbound container on the same Docker bridge.
                Install the secondary Pi-hole directly on a Raspberry Pi 3B with its own Unbound service on loopback.
                Each Pi-hole filters requests and sends public lookups to its own recursive resolver.
                Clients can use either server, so keep the local DNS records and filtering policy in sync and update one server at a time.
                Substitute addresses and hostnames from the target LAN in the examples below.
            </p>

            <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Primary Pi-hole on the NanoPi</h3>
            <p>
                Save the Compose file under <code>/opt/stacks/pihole</code>. The example pins the images to versions used in the lab.
                Pi-hole publishes DNS on TCP and UDP port 53 and its dashboard on port 8088, leaving ports 80 and 443 for the proxy.
                Unbound is reachable from Pi-hole on the Docker bridge. Pick a bridge subnet that does not overlap your LAN or VPN,
                and set <code>PIHOLE_PASSWORD</code> in a private <code>.env</code> file next to <code>docker-compose.yml</code>.
                See the <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/docker/">Pi-hole Docker guide</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-yaml">services:
  pihole:
    image: pihole/pihole:2026.11.0
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - "192.168.50.1:53:53/tcp"
      - "192.168.50.1:53:53/udp"
      - "192.168.50.1:8088:80/tcp"
    environment:
      TZ: Europe/London
      FTLCONF_webserver_api_password: "${PIHOLE_PASSWORD:?Set PIHOLE_PASSWORD in .env}"
      FTLCONF_dns_listeningMode: ALL
      FTLCONF_dns_upstreams: "172.30.53.2#53"
    volumes:
      - ./etc-pihole:/etc/pihole
    networks:
      dns:
    depends_on:
      - unbound
  unbound:
    image: klutchell/unbound:v1.26.2-20261119
    restart: unless-stopped
    networks:
      dns:
        ipv4_address: 172.30.53.2
networks:
  dns:
    ipam:
      config:
        - subnet: 172.30.53.0/24</code></pre>
            <p>
                The router’s LAN address needs TCP and UDP port 53 available before the stack starts.
                If dnsmasq currently serves DNS there, move its DNS listener or disable its DNS function while keeping DHCP enabled.
                Once both Pi-holes answer, set DHCP option <code>6,192.168.50.1,192.168.50.16</code> on the LAN in LuCI.
                Renew a client’s lease and check that it received both servers.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">cd /opt/stacks/pihole
chmod 600 .env
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker compose exec pihole pihole status</code></pre>
            <p>
                Before updating the Docker stack, export the Pi-hole settings with Teleporter and save the Compose file and persistent data off the router.
                Change the pinned image tag for the component being updated, then pull and recreate its container.
                For Unbound, use <code>unbound</code> as the service name in the pull and recreate commands. Check DNS after each update.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">docker compose config --quiet
docker compose pull pihole
docker compose up -d --no-deps pihole
docker compose exec pihole pihole version
docker compose logs --tail=30 pihole</code></pre>

            <h3 class="h5 mt-4">Secondary Pi-hole on the Raspberry Pi</h3>
            <p>
                Give the Pi a stable LAN address, such as <code>192.168.50.16</code>, and keep DHCP on FriendlyWrt.
                Install Unbound with APT and save the loopback configuration from the Unbound guide as <code>/etc/unbound/unbound.conf.d/pi-hole.conf</code>.
                Check that Unbound answers on port 5335 before running the native Pi-hole installer.
                See the <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/main/basic-install/">Pi-hole installation guide</a> and the
                <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/guides/dns/unbound/">Unbound configuration guide</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt update
sudo apt install unbound dnsutils

# After saving the Unbound configuration from the guide
sudo unbound-checkconf
sudo systemctl enable --now unbound
sudo systemctl restart unbound
dig @@127.0.0.1 -p 5335 example.org

curl -fsSL https://install.pi-hole.net -o basic-install.sh
less basic-install.sh
sudo bash basic-install.sh
sudo pihole-FTL --config dns.upstreams '["127.0.0.1#5335"]'
sudo systemctl restart pihole-FTL
sudo pihole status</code></pre>
            <p>
                Before updating the secondary server, export its Pi-hole settings. Use the native updater for Pi-hole and APT for Unbound.
                See the <a href="https://docs.pi-hole.net/main/pihole-command/#update">Pi-hole update command</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">sudo pihole -up
sudo apt update
sudo apt install --only-upgrade unbound
sudo systemctl is-active pihole-FTL unbound</code></pre>
            <p>
                Add the same local record to each Pi-hole, pointing the example hostname to the reverse proxy on the NanoPi.
                From another LAN machine, query both DNS servers directly over UDP and TCP, and check that each returns the same address for the local name.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">for dns in 192.168.50.1 192.168.50.16; do
    dig "@@$dns" example.org
    dig "@@$dns" +tcp example.org
    dig "@@$dns" app.home.arpa +short
done</code></pre>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Nginx Proxy Manager on the NanoPi</h2>
            <p>
                Nginx Proxy Manager (NPM) gives internal services readable hostnames and terminates public HTTPS for the CodeBuildDeploy dev sites.
                Run NPM in host-network mode so it can reach the private Istio frontend in Azure Kubernetes Service over the router’s WireGuard tunnel.
                The proxy listens on ports 80 and 443. Restrict access to the admin interface on port 81 to the LAN.
                See the <a href="https://nginxproxymanager.com/setup/">Nginx Proxy Manager setup guide</a> and
                <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/drivers/host/">Docker host networking</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Create a separate Compose stack under <code>/opt/stacks/npm</code>. The <code>data</code> directory contains the configuration and SQLite database,
                and <code>letsencrypt</code> stores certificate material.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-yaml">services:
  npm:
    image: jc21/nginx-proxy-manager:2.17.1
    network_mode: host
    restart: unless-stopped
    volumes:
      - ./data:/data
      - ./letsencrypt:/etc/letsencrypt</code></pre>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">cd /opt/stacks/npm
docker compose config --quiet
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps
docker compose exec npm nginx -t</code></pre>
            <p>
                In the admin UI, create a proxy host, enter its hostname and backend address, and select the certificate on the SSL tab.
                To mirror the dev sites, select HTTPS and port 443 for the private upstream. In the Advanced tab, set the upstream TLS server name and enable certificate verification.
                Replace <code>dev.example.org</code> with the name on the upstream certificate for that proxy host. See the
                <a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_ssl_name">Nginx upstream TLS directives</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-nginx">proxy_ssl_server_name on;
proxy_ssl_name dev.example.org;
proxy_ssl_verify on;
proxy_ssl_verify_depth 3;
proxy_ssl_trusted_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt;</code></pre>
            <p>
                Before upgrading, read the release notes and stop NPM. Back up the Compose file and both data directories together so the SQLite database
                and certificate files can be restored as a set. Run these commands as the stack administrator and copy the backup off the router.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">cd /opt/stacks/npm
umask 077
backup="$HOME/npm-before-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).tar.gz"
docker compose stop npm
tar -czf "$backup" docker-compose.yml data letsencrypt
tar -tzf "$backup"</code></pre>
            <p>
                Once the backup checks out, change the pinned image tag, pull the new image and recreate the service.
                If the backup fails, bring the existing service back with <code>docker compose start npm</code> and resolve the backup issue before upgrading.
                See the <a href="https://nginxproxymanager.com/upgrading/">Nginx Proxy Manager upgrade guide</a>.
            </p>
            <pre><code class="language-bash">docker compose config --quiet
docker compose pull npm
docker compose up -d --no-deps npm
docker compose exec npm nginx -t
docker compose logs --tail=30 npm

# From a LAN client, test HTTPS at the proxy using the real site name
curl --fail --silent --show-error --output /dev/null \
    --resolve dev.example.org:443:192.168.50.1 https://dev.example.org/</code></pre>
            <p>
                For a public site, repeat the HTTPS check from outside the home network using its public hostname without <code>--resolve</code>.
                This tests public DNS, the edge certificate, the WireGuard path and the application response together.
            </p>
        </div>
